  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/1/1c/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_97.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Thor saves a military pilot who's blacked out due to lack of oxygen, while at the same time ensuring that the faulty plane he's flying goes down away from the city.\n\nReturning to the medical offices of Dr. Don Blake, oblivious to the corner newsboy's hawking of a \"strange monster of the volcano,\" Blake finally makes his way inside.&nbsp; It's his day off, but he finds Jane Foster inside.&nbsp; She's there taking inventory of his supplies.\n\nAt that point, he's overwhelmed with the urge to confess to Jane how he feels about her, suggesting to her that he has something important to say.&nbsp; Jane can sense that he's on the verge of confessing his feelings for her, even going so far as to tell him to say it.&nbsp; Why <i>won't</i> he?&nbsp; Blake even calls her \"my darling,\" but in the end, he says that there's \"one thing I must do first\" (meaning that he must ask Odin's permission to reveal his secret).&nbsp; She replies, \"There is <i>always</i> just one more thing!&nbsp; Well, a girl can't wait forever... not for a man who hasn't enough gumption to speak his own mind!\"&nbsp; And with that, she walks out.\n\nBlake can't stand it anymore and decides to transform into Thor so that he can contact Odin and settle the matter once and for all.&nbsp; But when he asks his father for permission to \"marry a mortal girl,\" Odin's only response is \"Have you taken leave of your <i>senses</i>?\" and flatly refuses his petition.&nbsp; <i>Poof!</i>  He's gone!\n\nThor is saddened by his father's summary verdict, while remaining completely oblivious to the rising commotion on the streets below his office, where it seems <i>everyone</i> is holding a newspaper, reading about the menace of the mysterious \"Lava Man!\"\n\nMeanwhile, in another part of Asgard, still unable to leave, the evil Loki watches all, wanting to strike while his brother's heart is heavy with grief, but since he still can't leave, he looks for a \"catspaw, an agent to do the task for me!\"&nbsp; As the city's newspapers continue to print headlines about a \"Menace From Volcano Identified as Lava Man!\" Loki decides this will be the perfect agent for him to defeat Thor.\n\nBack in his identity as Don Blake, the doctor wrestles with his feelings about Odin's denial, even wondering if it might not be better for him to renounce his role as Thor.&nbsp; In the next moment, Jane once again shows up, but she's come only to announce that she's quitting her job at his practice -- to go work for another doctor!&nbsp; She can't <i>take</i> the uncertainty anymore and hates that \"Don\" won't speak his mind.&nbsp; Blake's only response is, \"Then -- this is goodbye, Jane.\"\n\nAs Blake walks alone on the street, dejected, the Lava Man strikes, claiming the dry surface of the Earth for the Lava People!&nbsp; Realizing there's a more urgent problem to solve, Thor soon appears.\n\nAs Thor and the Lava Man engage in battle, Thor can hear Loki's sinister laughter from above.&nbsp; A panel or two later, Thor notes that the city has been evacuated, and ultimately the struggle concludes with Thor forcing the Lava Man into a volcano and back down to his own people.\n\nThor returns his human identity to try to coax Jane to stay, but she is again upset that he didn't even try to find her while the city was threatened.&nbsp; She's done with him, and will now work for Dr. Bruce Andrews!&nbsp; \"A woman wants a <i>man</i>, Doctor Blake -- not a timid mouse!\"\n\nBlake stares sadly out the window...",
  "grade_one_liner": "A limp blend of overwrought romance and a hollow, obligatory Lava Man fight.\n<b>Buyer Beware</b>:&nbsp; This issue will drain you more than entertain.",
  "subtitle": "Bad Romance Meets Not-So-Hot Villain",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: display=block; textalign=center; severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=For thoughts on the <i>Tales of Asgard</i> backup featurette, see the Side Note below.]\n[DIVIDER]\nA very weak outing from <i>Journey Into Mystery</i>, which for this issue might have been better named\n[SPAN: display=block; textalign=center; fontsize=extralarge; severity=info; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Descent into Unrequited Romance].\nJane did everything short of telling Blake about her feelings, with him even slipping \"my darling\" into the dialogue as well.&nbsp; But to follow that up with him <i>still</i> struggling to announce his feelings -- that seems to have been the last straw for her.\n\nIt's a remarkably abrupt escalation, especially given how <i>little progress</i> their relationship ever seems to make.&nbsp; And then she actually says to him that \"a girl can't wait forever\" and suggests he \"hasn't enough gumption to speak his own mind!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=<i>Seriously</i>, I had to check the front cover to see if I'd perhaps opened a romance book by mistake...]\n\nI also find it hard to swallow that a nurse speaks this way to her employer.&nbsp; She is on the doctor's <i>staff</i> (being his only employee from the look of it).&nbsp; She may harbor amorous feelings for the good doctor, but why is someone who is <i>paid</i> to be there required to work for a \"man\" rather than a \"mouse?\"&nbsp; Her professional obligations shouldn't hinge on Blake's assertiveness, yet the story frames her frustration as if it's a job requirement.\n\nIf she struggles to do her job because she's fallen in love with her boss, that would argue for her leaving to go work somewhere else.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Does Dr. Andrews simply <i>pay</i> better?]\n\n[ASSET: right | MyDarling | width=27%] And let's not forget about the <i>reverse</i>.&nbsp; Reading this in the 21<sup>st</sup> century and seeing a doctor call his nurse \"my darling\" comes across as [SPAN: severity=error; text=extremely inappropriate], landing <i>quite</i> differently now compared to the completely innocent way it was likely intended back in 1963.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=To be fair:]&nbsp; the innocence is <i>still visible on the page</i>, but the power imbalance reads <i>far louder</i> now.\n[BR]\n[BR] (I talked about a similar innocence-versus-shock moment back in last summer's [LINK:1962:TTA_36_ant0|TTA #36], where Ant-Man climbs out of a woman's purse in her apartment -- [SPAN: severity=error; text=and she doesn't bat an eye!]&nbsp; <b><i>Wow!</i></b>)&nbsp; This scene plays the same way -- very <i>differently</i> when viewed through modern eyes.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nI got the biggest kick out of the middle panel on page 5, the one that shows just about everyone on the streets below Dr. Blake's office standing around reading a newspaper -- all conveniently positioned so that we readers could make out the headlines.\n[ASSET: center | StandingAround | width=67%]\n\nI <i>understand</i> this was simply a storytelling device to keep the mounting threat in the consciousness of the readers, but <i>seriously?</i>  [SPAN: severity=warning; text= Was <i>anyone</i> watching where they were going?]&nbsp; I counted at least <i>six</i> people just standing there holding newspapers in front of their faces, while another guy selling them shouted out \"<i>Extra</i>!&nbsp; More about <i>Lava Man</i>!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=I actually <i>laughed</i> at the unintended comedy of the bizarre scene.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Note also that <i>every single man</i> is drawn wearing a hat, but... that <i>was</i> simply the fashion of the day...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs for the actual <i>villain</i>, the cover advertises the coming of this new \"Lava Man\" character, even while the last panel on page 5 and first two on page 6 let us know that it was Loki who was responsible for him being on the surface of the Earth in the first place.&nbsp; From a storytelling perspective, I found this to be [SPAN: severity=error; text=completely unnecessary.]\n\nWhy was there a need for Loki to be involved at <i>all</i> with this threat?&nbsp; It's not like we're going to <i>forget</i> he's out there.&nbsp; We just saw him <i>twice</i> last month --once in [LINK:1963:AVEN_1|AVEN #1] (as its main villain) and again (albeit briefly) in the previous issue of <i>this</i> magazine<i>.</i>  Having him pop up here and claim responsibility for the Lava Man being on the surface is simply noise the story didn't need.\n\n[ASSET: right | LavaManArrives | width=20%] But even more to the point, the Lava Man's entire reason for being seems nothing short of an excuse for Jack Kirby to draw Thor battling someone.&nbsp; He just strolls into New York City, casually announcing that humans will have to \"Evacuate the cities!&nbsp; Take to the sea!\" [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Hey</i>, what will the <i>Sub-Mariner</i> have to say about that?)]\n[BR]\n[BR] There's no buildup, no mystery -- he simply appears and declares his intentions like he's reading from a cue card, waiting for the director to yell \"<b>CUT</b>!\"\n\nIt's almost like Jack was holding back for the first half of the story, impatiently waiting for all the <i>romance</i> to be over, all so that he could start drawing <i>action</i>.&nbsp; Well, here we <i>are</i> -- the Lava Man has arrived, and (as Stan would say) \"away we go!\"\n\nAnd indeed, off we went for the next six pages (spanning 7-13), as Thor and the Lava Man battled it out, with Stan occasionally using very poor grammar along the way.\n\nFor example, the first panel on page 8 has Thor saying \"The surface belongs to <i>mankind</i>, and <i>Thor</i> is [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=their] protector!!\" (I think he meant \"Thor is [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=its] protector.\")\n\nTo which the Lava Man replies, \"Your words [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=is] as futile as your deeds...\" (I think he meant \"Your words [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=are] as futile as your deeds...\")\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=This is nothing less than sloppy writing which shows that <i>everyone</i> (even Stan Lee) needs an editor.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And what's with the <i>letterer</i>?&nbsp; Couldn't Artie Simek be bothered to fix such <i>obvious</i> mistakes?)]\n\nI know this is nitpicking, <i>but still.</i>.. aren't these guys supposed to be <i>professionals?</i>  [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=Come on, man!]\n[BR] The mistakes aren't charming;&nbsp; they're <i>distracting</i>, especially in a book that's otherwise trying to sell high drama.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | CityEvacuated | width=14%] As for the battle, the one thing that stood out for me was the fact that, at the top of page 9, Thor indicates, \"The city has been evacuated!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] I feel compelled to point out that the Lava Man appeared on the city streets only [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=eight panels before]!&nbsp; There's no way the city could have cleared out that fast unless everyone was already halfway to the exits.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=I'm sorry folks, but <i>that has <b>got </b>to be the fastest evacuation in recorded history</i>!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=It's comic‑book logistics at their <b>most optimistic</b>!]\n\nMind you, this isn't even the <i>first</i> time the city was evacuated (see last year's [LINK:1962:FF_4|FF #4]).&nbsp; And don't forget that in last <i>month's </i>[LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1], they <i>wanted</i> to evacuate the city, but it was overrun too quickly -- that <i>Sub-Mariner</i> dude has caused some <i>serious</i> trouble!\n\nBut getting back to the battle, I found it one of the most yawn-worthy experiences I've had to endure.&nbsp; It seems that this Lava Man villain existed <i>solely</i> so that there would be a fight scene for Kirby to draw.&nbsp; And yet, despite all that effort, the action feels perfunctory -- a mandatory quota rather than a climax.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=As far as I'm concerned, that is not a good enough reason for him to be there -- and it just didn't work.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | HeckSmoothLines | width=16%] [ASSET: right | HeckArt | width=17%] This issue was drawn by Kirby, but inked by Don Heck, who, I will confess, did a great job smoothing out some of Kirby's rough angles, particularly in the faces (e.g. top-right panel of page 3, not to mention the bottom one that shows Jane Foster inside the office).&nbsp; This isn't the first time Heck has inked over Kirby's work, and it's a pairing that I think works well.\n\nSome of the latter-half panels looked far more like Heck's work than Kirby's.&nbsp; With Jack having to do Thor, FF, the FF Annual, Avengers, and X-Men (not to mention just about all the cover art), it <i>could</i> be that he was too rushed to provide full pencils and had to rely on another to tidy things up.&nbsp; It has that feel to it.&nbsp; The result is a hybrid aesthetic -- part Kirby dynamism, part Heck refinement -- and frankly, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=more interesting than the story it's propping up.]\n\nAnd while I'm on the subject of art, I found it curious that, on both the cover and splash page, the letters of the text \"Lava Man\" appear to be covered by what looks more like <b><i>snow</i></b> than lava.&nbsp; It's a weird, perhaps <i>accidental</i> effect that I'm sure was <i>intended</i> to look like molten blobs of lava sliding down the letters, but in both cases, it's colored white (even though the Lava Man himself is not), making it look like snow-capped letters.&nbsp; It's an odd mismatch, especially for a character whose entire identity revolves around heat.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis issue suffered from a horrible case of split personality.\n\nIt seemed that Stan wanted to focus more on the Blake/Thor and Jane plotline.&nbsp; Even though Blake/Thor knows Odin refuses to let him reveal himself to Jane, he asks anyway that he be allowed to marry her.&nbsp; Did he expect a different answer this time?&nbsp; <b><i>Really</i></b>?&nbsp; The repetition makes Blake look naive rather than romantic, which undercuts the emotional stakes the story keeps insisting on.\n\nThe whole thing smacks of Stan Lee perhaps returning to write the monthly Thor issue after having <i>just penned</i> one of the romance comics of the day -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=<i>major</i> bleed-over -- <b><i>bleah</i></b>.]\n\nMeanwhile, artist Jack Kirby seemed to want to get on with the <i>action</i> (though Don Heck obviously contributed a lot as well).&nbsp; But while that consumed an entire half of the story, it was the <i>least</i> interesting part of the read, and I mostly yawned my way through it.&nbsp; The Lava Man shows up, causes the city <i>to be evacuated</i>, and is ultimately shoved back down a volcano by the title hero.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=Wake me up when it's over!]\n\n[ASSET: left | BrutalParting | width=21%] But outside of the useless battle panels that wasted the readers' time, the worse part of the whole thing was the conclusion back in Don Blake's office that had Jane showing up to say goodbye to him (bringing her new boss <i>with</i> her, no less).&nbsp; Not only that, but she can't resist the urge to twist the knife and express her disappointment that he didn't even try to find her during the recent crisis -- to make sure she was okay.\n[BR]\n[BR] \"A woman wants a <i>man</i>, Doctor Blake -- not a timid mouse!&nbsp; And so, I'm leaving...\"\n\nAdmittedly, that's [SPAN: severity=error; text=absolutely <i>brutal</i>] on her part, but it only goes to show how bankrupt the story is, as the entire \"I'm quitting\" plotline is sprung on readers without any hint dropped of such a thing being a possibility before.\n\nEven though I finally felt a little better after enduring the Lava Man sequence, I was immediately shoved back into what <i>preceded</i> it -- [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=oh yeah... <b>that!</b>]\n\nI could almost feel my life force draining away, as if it, too, was one of those molten blobs dripping off the page.\n\nIt was a draining end to a draining issue -- and <b>not</b> in the way anyone involved intended.",
  "side_note": [
    "[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=large; text=<b>Tales of Asgard</b>] makes its first appearance in this issue, and here's how it will be handled:\n\n<b><u>TEMPORARY SCENARIO-BREAKING EXPLANATION:</u></b>\n[BR] I will largely treat <i>Tales of Asgard </i>as a separate story, but its brevity and lack of general depth argue that it will not get its own grade.&nbsp; But <i>since it is part of the published issue</i>, if I feel it is <i>so good</i> or <i>so awful</i> to cause the main story's grade to be tweaked, I will indicate that in the Bottom Line section above (and make the appropriate adjustment).&nbsp; Think of that as another kind of <i>Backup Bail-out Award</i> and nothing else.\n\nIf there are significant first appearances that take place here, I will add that to the metadata for this entire book.&nbsp; (For example, I believe Sif makes her first appearance in one of these back-up features.)\n\nIn as much as these stories relate to Thor and the Asgardians, I will simply treat it as part of the same book.&nbsp; For the record, I plan to do the same thing with the <i>Origins of the X-Men</i> back-up feature that will appear at the tail end of <i>that</i> magazine (still years from now).\n\nOnce the back-up featurette in <i>Thor</i> transitions over to the <i>Inhumans</i>, those stories <i>will</i> get their own grades (as they have nothing to do with Thor).\n[BR] <b>END OF EXPLANATION</b>\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe inaugural story provides some background into the Norse legends, the good ones being the Aesir and the bad ones the Frost Giants, even mentioning the Ymir, the greatest of these Frost Giants, and Surtur.&nbsp; Buri was the first of the Aesir, who was the grandfather of Odin himself, greatest of all the Norse gods, who slayed the last of the Ice Giants.\n\nOdin and his two brothers protected Earth with a ring around the planet, blanked by the branches of Yggdrasil, the magic tree, which protected it \"while awaiting the coming of <i>man</i>!\"\n\nLee and Kirby presumably open up the Norse mythology texts and share some of its material, which we can only assume will be woven into the fabric of the Marvel/Norse gods.&nbsp; We'll see....&nbsp; We're promised more to come in the next issue!"
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "The Lava Man is one of the \"Lava Men,\" who next appear in <i>Avengers</i> #5, coming up in just a few short months.",
  "highlight": "Thor faces a Lava Man, loses Jane, and somehow the romance hurts more than the villain."
}